Classic Staff-level producer/consumer example

#include <condition_variable>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>

class Queue
{
public:
    void push(int value)
    {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            queue_.push(value);
        }

        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    int pop()
    {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);

        cv_.wait(lock, [this] {
            return !queue_.empty();
        });

        int value = queue_.front();
        queue_.pop();

        return value;
    }

private:
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    std::queue<int> queue_;
};



Producer:

std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);

Why?
It only needs:

lock
  ↓
modify queue
  ↓
unlock

No special ownership manipulation.

Consumer:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
cv_.wait(lock, ...);

Why?
Because wait() needs to:

unlock → sleep → wake → relock


Interviewer's likely follow-up questions:

Q1. Why can't lock_guard be used with condition_variable::wait()?
Because wait() needs a lock object whose ownership can be released while waiting and reacquired after wakeup. lock_guard doesn't provide that interface.

Q2. Can unique_lock be copied?
No.
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> a(m);

// std::unique_lock<std::mutex> b = a; // ❌

But it can be moved:

auto b = std::move(a); // ✅

Q3. What happens to a after std::move(a)?
a remains a valid unique_lock object but no longer owns the mutex.

a.owns_lock() == false

while b owns it.

Q4. What does defer_lock do?
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::defer_lock);

Constructs the lock object without acquiring the mutex.
Later:
lock.lock();
can acquire it.

Q5. What does try_to_lock do?
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::try_to_lock);

Attempts to acquire without blocking.
Check:
lock.owns_lock();

Q6. Why use std::scoped_lock instead of std::lock_guard?
For multiple mutexes:

std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);

It provides RAII while safely locking multiple mutexes.


Q7. What happens if two threads acquire two mutexes in opposite order?
Potential deadlock:
T1: lock(A) → lock(B)

T2: lock(B) → lock(A)

Use consistent lock ordering or:

std::scoped_lock lock(A, B);

or the equivalent std::lock + deferred unique_lock approach.



"What's the difference between lock_guard and unique_lock?"

"lock_guard is a simple RAII wrapper with fixed ownership semantics: it acquires the mutex during construction and releases it during destruction. 
It's ideal when the entire scope should be protected.
unique_lock is a more flexible ownership object. It can defer acquisition, try to acquire, explicitly unlock and relock, report ownership, and transfer 
ownership through move semantics. Most importantly, condition_variable::wait() requires a unique_lock because waiting involves atomically releasing the mutex and 
reacquiring it after wakeup.
So I use lock_guard when I need a simple lexical critical section, and unique_lock when the lock lifetime or ownership needs to be manipulated. In C++17, for locking multiple mutexes without needing manual ownership manipulation, I'd also consider scoped_lock."
